{"id":"tax-refund-settlements","title":"Tax handed back by settlement","question":"Which tax-refund claims has the City settled, for how much, and is the total growing?","finding":"Since fiscal 2015 the Board has approved 114 settlements of tax-refund claims naming $262.6 million: $10.4 million in the six years to fiscal 2020 and $252.2 million since. The largest: General Motors Company ($71.1 million, 2026); Stripe, Inc. ($49.0 million, 2025); Twilio Inc. ($18.0 million, 2023); WeWork Companies Inc. ($13.3 million, 2023); Levi’s Plaza TIC Trust ($10.2 million, 2021). 8 of the files settle a property-tax suit by agreeing an assessed value, with or without a stated refund.","how":"Board files titled as settlements whose text speaks of a refund or overpayment of tax, or of a stipulated assessed value, passed since July 2014 and in which the City pays. The amount is the figure in the title; for a stipulated assessed value it is the refund stated, if any, and never the property value. “About” is the file’s own description, cut to 25 words. Largest first. Firms are named as the file names them; private persons are not named.","limits":"Every one of these was voted on by the Board, and a settlement of a refund claim is an end to a dispute over what tax was owed: it says nothing about the taxpayer’s conduct. They are here because they are money handed back, and because the total has grown; the largest files name the gross receipts, homelessness gross receipts, overpaid executive and commercial rents taxes. The title gives the principal; several add statutory interest that is not counted. Refunds paid without a claim or lawsuit that reached the Board are not in these files. What would supply those: the Treasurer and Tax Collector’s record of refunds. Amounts, parties and dates taken from Board file titles are read by machine: in a hand check of 50 titles, 11 had at least one field read wrongly (4 after later fixes).","tier":"OFFICIAL","columns":[{"k":"party","t":"Taxpayer",